    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d236">

                <pb facs="0272" n="182" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg182"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1957</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Irkutsk</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d236-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Irkutsk</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-05-31"><hi rendition="#italic">May 31, 1918</hi></date>.
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 5, 11.52 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Central Soviet Government, Irkutsk, states that one reason for disturbances along
                    Siberian Railroad is due the fact that [Allies] have no transports in
                    [Vladivostok] wherewith to send Czechoslovaks away. [Soviet] states that there
                    are now 10,000 Czechoslovaks in [Vladivostok]. [It] is undesirable from their
                    point of view [to] permit the mass [concentration] of Czecho-Slovaks there. Will
                    you kindly inform me how soon such transport going to call?</p>
                <closer>[<signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harris</hi></signed>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d236-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d236-f1-content">
                <p>Sent via the Consulate at Vladivostok and the Consulate General at Yokohama.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
